\title{The Cedar unit that blew up under the wrong \code{\verb~|let~} rule}
\taxon{Example}
\date{2026-11-24}
\author{claude-fable-5-2}
\contributor{queclr}
\tag{machine}
\tag{nat}
\tag{performance}
\meta{origin}{docs/design/RECONCILIATION.md}
\import{base-macros}

\p{Unit 7008 of the Cedar slice, \code{\verb~|BitVec.neg_toInt_eq_neg_64._proof_1_1~}, is a certificate of the \code{\verb~|omega~} tactic whose definitions bind \code{\verb~|let~}s. Under the rule of the instruction-set document it ran into the memory limit; under the corrected rule it takes 38 ms. Source: erratum E1 of \src{docs/design/RECONCILIATION.md}, section 5, and decision D9 of \src{rlvm-machine/NOTES-M2.md}; [the session on the machine](ses-Z72N). The code is a schematic of the shape, not the text of the unit.}
\pre{\verb~|
-- body of a definition that the kernel unfolds while it evaluates
def step (x y : Nat) : Nat :=
  let q := x / y        -- x, y literals near 2^63 in the certificate
  let r := x % y
  gcd y r

-- rule of the design document: q and r are LET suspensions, so every value built
--   from them is OPEN ("has a free variable"); lazy delta then does not fold
--   Nat.div, Nat.mod, gcd on literals but unfolds them by their fuel recursion
-- official whnf_core: instantiate the body with the value; no variable; literals fold
~}
\table{
\tr{\th{}\th{Machine steps}\th{Outcome}}
\tr{\td{every \code{\verb~|let~} value a LET suspension}\td{73.7 M}\td{memory decline}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|let~} as zeta by sharing}\td{60,569}\td{38 ms; the reference engine takes 42 ms}}
}
\ul{
\li{Officially a let variable is a free variable only inside \code{\verb~|infer_let~}. For a \code{\verb~|let~} inside a term that is evaluated, \code{\verb~|whnf_core~} instantiates the body with the value.}
\li{Five Cedar declarations ran at about 1,000 times the official work under the wrong rule.}
}
\p{Used by [a let must be zeta by sharing](ins-H55H).}
\p{See also: [the design review and its reconciliation](design-review-reconciliation).}
